@node Customizations
@chapter Customizations

SSS supports several user customizations, more specifically:

Several color palettes, inspired by the great `ef-themes` by Protesilaos Stavrou, including @code{ef-bio}, @code{ef-cyprus} and @code{ef-dream}.

Find Prot's themes here: @uref{https://github.com/protesilaos/ef-themes}

@node Per host - per-hostscm
@chapter Per host - per-host.scm

It is @strong{REQUIRED} to include a @code{per-host.scm} in the root of this project, which is excluded from Git, and will determine certain settings for your own machine.
Find here a reference configuration with what is required.

@example
(use-modules (gnu)
             (gnu packages))

(define sss-filesystems
  (list (file-system
         (device "/dev/nvme0n1p3")
         (mount-point "/")
         (type "ext4"))
        (file-system
         (device "/dev/nvme0n1p1")
         (mount-point "/boot/efi")
         (type "vfat"))))

;; packages that should only be installed in the current host
;; or (define sss-per-host-packages '())
(define sss-per-host-packages
  (list
   ;; AMD non-free drivers
   (specification->package "amd-microcode")
   (specification->package "amdgpu-firmware")
   ;; Games
   (specification->package "prismlauncher")
   (specification->package "steam")))

;; location where you cloned SSS Git repository
(define sss-clone-dir
  "/home/joe/Ontwikkeling/Persoonlijk/sss")

;;
;; Define the active palette for all users accounts
;; You should reconfigure and ideally restart the system
;; for all changes to take effect
;;
;; Note: You could choose on a per-user basis to set a fixed palette
;; and not follow system wide, by just redefining this value
;;
;; Possible values are:
;;   - sss-palette-ef-bio
;;   - sss-palette-ef-cyprus
;;   - sss-palette-ef-dream
;;
(define-public sss-active-palette
  'sss-palette-ef-dream)

@end example

@node Bootstrapping
@chapter Bootstrapping

It's possible you need to some manual installations, and temporary workarounds, in order to install @code{sss} on a brand-new Guix installation.

Some aspects will be dependent on the manner of installation and hardware. Below follows a simple startup guide.

Download and the Guix GNU/Linux distribution from the official Guix page: @uref{https://guix.gnu.org/download/} and make a bootable installation medium convenient for your use case.

If you are not familiar with the @code{dd} command which is present in all GNU/Linux distributions and in macOS, you can always feel free to use something like Rufus on Windows, or balenaEtcher, to flash this image into a USB, with which you can then boot your computer.

It is highly recommended to first become familiar with SSS and Guix via a @emph{throw-away} virtual machine where you can experiment and do mistakes. After familiarity is acquired then a bare metal installation is the best.

SSS strives to be completely cross-architecture and should work well everywhere. That being said, as of the latest manual, x86-64 is the preferred architecture.

If you are on aarch64/arm64, or other more niche architectures, then you might need to put in some more work to get an installer image, and to get it working, likely having to generate an ISO image yourself to install Guix or a qcow2 virtual machine.

The following articles may be of help:
@uref{https://jointhefreeworld.org/blog/articles/gnu-linux/gnu-guix-virtual-machine-image-aarch64/}

When installing Guix, make sure you take note of the Scheme code that gets generated by it, specially for the disk partitioning. This will later need to be added to the @code{per-host.scm}.

After having installed things using the guided Guix installer, or via the command line for advanced users, boot into your new system.

I would recommend to then add @code{nonguix} in your channels file (@samp{$HOME/.config/guix/channels.scm}).

@example
(cons* (channel
      (name 'nonguix)
      (url "https://gitlab.com/nonguix/nonguix")
      (introduction
       (make-channel-introduction
        "897c1a470da759236cc11798f4e0a5f7d4d59fbc"
        (openpgp-fingerprint
         "2A39 3FFF 68F4 EF7A 3D29 12AF 6F51 20A0 22FB B2D5"))))
     %default-channels)

@end example

After adding the channel, it is then highly recommended to perform a @code{guix pull} from your user account (@strong{no sudo or root}) and let all channels get updated.

You should every now and then do a @code{guix pull} from your regular user, and then rebuild this system with the root user (@code{make sr}). Guix is a rolling distribution and you don't need to be always on bleeding edge releases, but it's nice to stay updated.

Once you have followed all those above steps, you can enter a temporary Guix shell, so as to bootstrap SSS@.

You can do this with @code{guix shell make git}.

Then proceed to clone SSS with @code{git clone https://codeberg.org/jjba23/sss.git}.

You should have by now written an appropriate @code{per-host.scm} for your setup. See the above section on this topic.

Enter the directory of SSS: @code{cd sss}.

Make sure you place your @code{per-host.scm} in the root of this directory. Then you can do a @code{make sr}.

This translates to @code{sudo guix system reconfigure config.scm --fallback}.

For more commands take a look at the SSS Makefile: @uref{../../Makefile}.

The @code{fallback} option is optional, and simply helps when upstream substitute (cache) servers are less available.

Once the system reconfigure is complete, you should also bootstrap your user's GNU home with something like: @code{guix home reconfigure ./home/joe.scm  --fallback}

You can then reboot and you should be greeted by a simple TTY@.

SSS uses @strong{no login managers} like GDM or SDDM@. Simply login to the TTY and start your favorite GUI (or not). I like to alias this to @code{gui}, or sometimes I directly run @code{sway} from the TTY@.

Feel free to contact me for any doubts at jjbigorra@@gmail.com.

@node Nix profile
@chapter Nix profile

Nixpkgs contains a lot of software which we can leverage and manage from Guix.

SSS will automatically install and provide the Nix package manager for you.

SSS also provides some facilities to manage Nix from the comfort of your Guile Scheme.

You might need to activate and link the profile in order to be able to use, for the first time.

Assuming @code{joe} as user in question.

@code{/nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/} should be:
@example
joe@@guixvm ~ λ  ll /nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/
totaal 20K
drwxr-xr-x 2 joe  users  16K 29 dec 23:09 joe/
drwxr-xr-x 2 root root  4,0K 22 nov 13:23 root/
@end example

For this, make the directory an link it to your home.

@example
sudo mkdir -p /nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/joe/profile
sudo chown -R /nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/joe
ln -sfv /nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/joe/profile /home/joe/.nix-profile
@end example

It's possible you might need to logout and log back in to re-activate the profile.

You can switch your install to use nixpkgs-unstable with:

@example
nix-channel --add https://nixos.org/channels/nixpkgs-unstable
nix-channel --update
@end example

You can install Nix packages by running:

@example
nix -L profile install --impure nixpkgs#firefox
@end example

You can keep your Nix packages updated by running:

@example
NIXPGS_ALLOW_UNFREE=1 nix profile upgrade --impure '.*'
@end example

The installed software will be available, for example at: @code{/home/joe/.nix-profile/bin/}

